e surfaces in contact and the normal force (the force perpendicular to the surfaces).\n4. The normal force is directly proportional to the weight of the object. As the weight of the object increases, the normal force also increases.\n5. The relationship between the normal force and the frictional force is given by the equation: Frictional force = coefficient of friction \u00d7 normal force.\n6. The coefficient of friction is a constant value that depends on the nature of the surfaces in contact. It is usually denoted by the Greek letter mu (\u03bc).\n7. Since the normal force increases with the weight of the object, and the frictional force is directly proportional to the normal force, we can conclude that the relationship between the amount of friction and the weight of the object is direct.", "new_cot_probs": [2.015193220628042e-11, 8.94236594495279e-12, 1.721093489948089e-08, 3.764876604916978e-11], "cot_prob": [-1.125], "cot_step_prob": [-1.171875]}}}
